# /etc/ttyd-ify/config — read by wt-serve. Shell syntax (KEY=value).
#
# ⚠  ttyd-ify serves a WRITABLE shell over the network, UNAUTHENTICATED unless you set
#    WT_PASSWORD below. Bind it only to a trusted interface, password or not. NEVER set WT_BIND to 0.0.0.0 and
#    NEVER open a firewall port to it. See the README SECURITY section.
#
# install.sh writes this file 0640 root:$WT_USER — root-owned, readable only by the
# account the service runs as, because WT_BIND is an access control and WT_PASSWORD is a
# secret. If you change the service user, RE-RUN THE INSTALL: the file would
# otherwise be unreadable by the new one, and wt-serve refuses to start rather than fall
# back to defaults you did not configure.

# Where to bind. One of:
#   tailscale   -> this node's Tailscale IP (tailscale ip -4)   [recommended for tailnet use]
#   localhost   -> 127.0.0.1 only (reach via SSH tunnel / reverse proxy)
#   <iface>     -> an interface name, e.g. tailscale0, eth0, wg0
#   <ip>        -> a literal address to bind
#
# This value has to resolve on the machine you are installing on. install.sh resolves it up front
# and REFUSES rather than installing a service that cannot bind — a fresh box with no tailscale
# would otherwise get this file's default, a server that exits, and a success message (#80). If
# that refusal is what brought you here: bring tailscale up ('tailscale ip -4' must print an
# address), or set this to localhost, an interface name, or an address this box holds.
WT_BIND=tailscale

# Port the server listens on. One box, one listener, one port.
WT_PORT=7681

# Where session sockets live. Commented out on purpose: unset means the service user's
# ~/.dtach, which wtd resolves itself, so this file does not have to know who that is.
# Set it only to move sessions elsewhere, and keep it short — <dir>/<name>.sock has to fit
# in 107 bytes or nothing can connect to the sessions created there.
#WT_DIR=/srv/sessions

# Project-shortcut file (see etc/projects.example).
WT_PROJECTS=/etc/ttyd-ify/projects

# Path to the wtd binary (built by `make build`, or a release download).
WT_WEB_BIN=/usr/local/bin/wtd

# How much recent output wtd replays when a client attaches to a session, per session.
#
# This is what makes reattaching show context instead of a blank screen: wtd holds its own
# attachment to each deep-linked session and remembers the tail. Leave it unset to take
# wtd's default (256 KiB). Set it to 0 to switch replay off entirely — sessions still work,
# they just come back blank until something writes. Note that is blanker than it was before
# replay existed, for the browser page only: its own redraw kick is gone, and the hub kicks
# once when it first attaches rather than on every connect. iOS builds still kick themselves.
# WT_REPLAY_BYTES=262144

# The server password. OFF unless set, and then EVERY route needs it — terminals, /ws, the
# session API and /files/ — except /healthz. A browser signs in once on a login page and
# keeps a 30-day cookie; the iOS app and curl send `Authorization: Bearer <password>`.
#
# Plaintext here, on purpose: this file is 0640 root:<service user>, and anyone who can read
# it already has the shell it protects. The server REFUSES TO START if it is set but shorter
# than 12 characters, or holds anything but printable ASCII, or starts or ends with a space
# (WT_PASSWORD= with nothing after it is set, and refused). Quote it if it contains spaces or
# shell characters:  WT_PASSWORD='correct horse battery staple'
#
# Browsers scope cookies by host, not port: a signed-in browser also sends its wtd cookie to
# every other HTTP service at this box's address (dev servers, k3s NodePorts), which could
# replay it against wtd. To sign every browser out, change the password, or delete
# /run/wt/session.key, and restart.
#
# It does not replace WT_BIND. It is the second line, for a network you share.
#WT_PASSWORD=

# Read-only file and markdown browser at /files/ on the same port. OFF unless WT_FILES_ROOT
# is set, and when it is set the server REFUSES TO START unless WT_PASSWORD is set too.
#WT_FILES_ROOT=/home/you/src

# Where files uploaded from the terminal page's upload button land. Default ~/uploads (the
# service user's home), created on first upload. Absolute path. A name that is already taken
# gets -1, -2, ... before its extension: an upload never overwrites a file.
#WT_UPLOAD_DIR=/home/you/uploads

# ── retired keys ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ttyd used to serve the web page; wtd replaced it and now owns WT_PORT. Three keys went
# with ttyd, and a fourth with the /files/-only password. They are listed here because your
# config may still have them — install.sh never clobbers /etc/ttyd-ify/config — and because
# some of them are security settings whose loss must not be silent:
#
#   WT_AUTH        ttyd's HTTP basic auth (user:pass). Both the install and the server
#                  REFUSE TO START if it is non-empty, rather than guess what you meant:
#                  move the password to WT_PASSWORD above and clear this line.
#   WT_TTYD_ARGS   Raw ttyd flags. There is no ttyd left to pass them to, and they could
#                  carry -c or -R, so this also refuses to start rather than drop a
#                  restriction you configured.
#   WT_WEB_PORT    wtd's own port while both servers ran side by side. Warned about and
#                  ignored; WT_PORT is the port. Delete the line.
#   WT_FILES_PASSWD  The bcrypt file that gated /files/ alone. WT_PASSWORD replaced it and
#                  covers /files/ too. Warned about and ignored; delete the line and the file.
